WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.526
A power of attorney lets someone you trust

2
00:00:03.526 --> 00:00:06.328
handle your affairs if you can't.

3
00:00:06.328 --> 00:00:11.100
It's one of the most useful documents you can sign.

4
00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:15.566
A durable one keeps working if you become incapacitated.

5
00:00:15.566 --> 00:00:18.600
That is when it matters most.

6
00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.095
Your agent can often pay bills,

7
00:00:21.095 --> 00:00:23.202
manage accounts, file taxes,

8
00:00:23.202 --> 00:00:24.893
and deal with property,

9
00:00:24.893 --> 00:00:27.600
within the powers you give.

10
00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:30.499
They can't make a will for you,

11
00:00:30.499 --> 00:00:33.627
and the authority ends when you die.

12
00:00:33.627 --> 00:00:36.532
And they must act in your interest,

13
00:00:36.532 --> 00:00:38.600
not their own.

14
00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:42.407
Some powers, like making large gifts or changing

15
00:00:42.407 --> 00:00:45.964
your trust, usually need to be spelled out.

16
00:00:45.964 --> 00:00:48.625
Banks may ask for their own forms,

17
00:00:48.625 --> 00:00:50.600
so plan ahead.

18
00:00:50.825 --> 00:00:53.700
Pick carefully, and name a backup.

19
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.145
Our guide has the questions to ask.
